.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT
.Dd October 12, 2026
.Dt DTFL_ALGO_BFS 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm dtfl_algo_bfs ,
.Nm dtfl_algo_scc ,
.Nm dtfl_algo_propagate ,
.Nm dtfl_algo_sequence ,
.Nm dtfl_algo_bidijkstra ,
.Nm dtfl_algo_identifiers ,
.Nm dtfl_algo_prefix_sum
.Nd graph and sequence algorithms on differential dataflow
.Sh LIBRARY
Timely and differential dataflow library
.Pq libdtfl , \-ldtfl
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In dtfl/algo.h
.Ft dtfl_collection *
.Fn dtfl_algo_bfs "dtfl_collection *edges" "dtfl_collection *roots"
.Ft dtfl_collection *
.Fn dtfl_algo_scc "dtfl_collection *graph"
.Ft dtfl_collection *
.Fn dtfl_algo_propagate "dtfl_collection *edges" "dtfl_collection *nodes"
.Ft dtfl_collection *
.Fn dtfl_algo_sequence "dtfl_collection *state" "dtfl_collection *edges" "dtfl_algo_sequence_fn logic" "dtfl_ctx ctx"
.Ft dtfl_collection *
.Fn dtfl_algo_bidijkstra "dtfl_collection *edges" "dtfl_collection *goals"
.Ft dtfl_collection *
.Fn dtfl_algo_identifiers "dtfl_collection *c" "const dtfl_type *rec_type"
.Ft dtfl_collection *
.Fn dtfl_algo_prefix_sum "dtfl_collection *c" "uint64_t zero" "dtfl_algo_combine_fn combine" "dtfl_ctx ctx"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These functions assemble common graph and sequence algorithms from the
differential dataflow collection operators
.Pq Xr dtfl_collection_join 3 , Xr dtfl_collection_reduce 3 , Xr dtfl_collection_iterate 3 .
Each takes and returns
.Vt dtfl_collection
handles over fixed record layouts of 64-bit node identifiers: a node is a
.Vt u64
record, an edge is a pair of two
.Vt u64 ,
and a labelled node is a pair of two
.Vt u64 .
Every input difference class is the signed default
.Pq Xr dtfl_diff_i64 3 ;
the output carries the same class.
None of these functions takes ownership of its inputs: the caller still
frees them, as with the other collection methods.
.Pp
.Fn dtfl_algo_bfs
labels each node reachable from a root with its minimum distance.
.Fa edges
is a collection of
.Pq Vt u64 src , Vt u64 dst
edge records and
.Fa roots
is a collection of bare
.Vt u64
node records; the result is a collection of
.Pq Vt u64 node , Vt u32 dist
records, a pair of a
.Vt u64
and a
.Vt u32
.Pq twelve bytes .
It seeds each root at distance zero, then iterates to a fixed point: join
each labelled node against its outgoing edges to reach each destination at
distance plus one, concatenate the roots back in, and reduce to keep the
minimum distance per node.
The distance counter is a
.Vt u32 ,
kept as upstream keeps it.
.Pp
.Fn dtfl_algo_scc
returns the subset of
.Fa graph
edges whose endpoints share a strongly connected component, with the same
.Pq Vt u64 src , Vt u64 dst
record layout.
It iterates a variable seeded from the whole edge set, trimming it twice
each round, once by forward label propagation and once on the transposed
graph; an edge survives a trim when both endpoints carry the same minimum
label.
.Pp
.Fn dtfl_algo_propagate
assigns each node the minimum label reachable along directed edges.
.Fa edges
is a collection of
.Pq Vt u64 src , Vt u64 dst
edge records and
.Fa nodes
is a collection of
.Pq Vt u64 node , Vt u64 label
records; the result has the same labelled-node layout.
It builds a monotonic iteration variable seeded empty, concatenates the
nodes, keeps the minimum label per node with a reduce, and flows labels
forward along the edges until a fixed point.
.Pp
.Fn dtfl_algo_sequence
applies
.Fa logic
to nodes in order of node identifier.
.Fa state
gives each node's initial
.Pq Vt u64 node , Vt u64 value ;
.Fa edges
are the directed edges.
A node fires once all of its lower-identifier neighbours have fired, and
.Fa logic
computes its new value from the accumulated neighbour values, sorted by
value, writing a
.Vt u64
through
.Fa out .
The result is a collection of
.Pq Vt u64 node , option Vt u64
records: None until the node fires, then Some of its value.
The user context in
.Fa ctx
must outlive the dataflow; the function does not take ownership of it.
.Pp
.Fn dtfl_algo_bidijkstra
performs a bidirectional search from both ends of each goal.
.Fa edges
are the directed edges and
.Fa goals
is a collection of
.Pq Vt u64 source , Vt u64 target
pairs; the result is a collection of
.Pq Pq Vt u64 source , Vt u64 target , Vt u32 dist
records giving the shortest distance of each goal whose source reaches its
target.
.Pp
.Fn dtfl_algo_identifiers
assigns each element of
.Fa c
a unique
.Vt u64
identifier derived from a hash, resolving collisions by round so every
element receives a distinct identifier.
.Fa rec_type
describes the records of
.Fa c ;
the result is a collection of
.Pq record , Vt u64 id
records.
.Pp
.Fn dtfl_algo_prefix_sum
computes a data-parallel prefix sum.
.Fa c
is a collection of
.Pq Pq Vt u64 index , Vt u64 key , Vt u64 data
records; each element's data becomes the combination of all data at the
same key with index at most its own.
.Fa zero
is the identity value and
.Fa combine
is the associative combiner, applied per key.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Each function returns a new
.Vt dtfl_collection
handle in the input's scope, which the caller frees with
.Fn dtfl_collection_free .
Allocation failure is reported through the panic hook, as elsewhere in the
library.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Compute distances from a root over an edge collection and read them with an
inspect:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
dtfl_collection *dist = dtfl_algo_bfs(edges, roots);
dtfl_collection *ins = dtfl_collection_inspect(dist, sink, ctx);
dtfl_collection *probed = dtfl_collection_probe_with(ins, probe);
/* step the worker, then read (node, u32 dist) records in sink */
.Ed
.Sh ERRORS
A join whose two difference classes have no declared multiply reports
.Dv DTFL_EALGEBRA
and the underlying operator returns NULL
.Pq Xr dtfl_collection_join 3 .
The default
.Vt i64
class these algorithms use multiplies with itself, so this does not arise
for their built-in record layouts.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dtfl_collection_iterate 3 ,
.Xr dtfl_collection_join 3 ,
.Xr dtfl_collection_new 3 ,
.Xr dtfl_collection_reduce 3
.Sh HISTORY
These functions port the differential dataflow
.Pa src/algorithms
module
.Pq bfs.rs , scc.rs , propagate.rs , sequential.rs , bijkstra.rs , identifiers.rs , prefix_sum.rs
from the pinned upstream
.Pq differential-dataflow-v0.25.1 .
.Sh THREAD SAFETY
Each function builds operators in the scope of its input collections and
belongs to the worker thread that owns that scope.
The collections it returns are worker-local handles that never leave their
worker thread, like every other collection handle.
Different workers build the same dataflow independently; records exchange
across workers through the arrangements the join and reduce operators
build, not through shared handles.
